From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps5sd <ps5sd@localhost>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] server: one shape for every error answer

The routes answered errors in at least four shapes: {"error": "text"} (most
of them and the application's routes), {"error": "server_error", "message":
...} out of a catch, an empty body from cpp-httplib (a route that does not
exist), and {"error": {"code", "message"}} in a failed job. A client had to
know them all, and a program could not tell an out-of-memory run from a full
queue but by the words.

Every answer of 400 and more is now {"error": {"code", "message"}}, as a
failed job has it and as the OpenAI API answers. install_error_shape()
(api_error.cpp) puts it into that shape on its way out, in cpp-httplib's
error handler, so the routes that still answer a text, an empty body or
plain text come out the same: the text becomes the message, the status the
code (404 not_found, 409 conflict, ...); a top-level "code" from the
application names it. The standby answer, the API key's answers, a full
queue and a failed generation name their own: standby, unauthorized,
rate_limited, queue_full, out_of_memory, generation_failed, cancelled. A
failed job's code says out_of_memory now too (sd_server_failure_code). A
body that is not JSON is 400 invalid_request instead of 500, and an
exception out of a route is 500 server_error with what was thrown.

